.TH SPLSTAT 1.SH NAMEsplman, splprt, splstat \- the OS-9 print spooler.SH SYNOPSIS\fBsplman &\fR.br\fBsplprt &\fR.br\fBsplstat\fR.SH DESCRIPTIONThree programs of one spooler, which keeps its queue in the file\fB/dd/SPL/splq\fRand its form definitions in\fB/dd/SPL/splforms\fR.\fR.TP 12\fBsplman\fRthe manager.  Started in the background it waits for work, asleepbetween looks at the queue, and prints nothing..TP 12\fBsplprt\fRthe printer process, one to a printer: it prints the jobs the managerhands it, and like the manager runs in the background..TP 12\fBsplstat\fRreports the queue as a table,\fBJob  User  Unit  Copies  Status  Forms\fR,\fRand the printers as\fBDev  Name  Status  Forms  File  Options\fR.\fRWith nothing queued it prints nothing and returns 0.  Changing a jobthat is not yours answers\fBYou do not own that job\fR;\fRchanging a printer wants the system manager..PPThe command that puts a job in the queue is not part of this set..SH FILES.TP 12\fB/dd/SPL/splq\fRthe queue, shipped empty..SH NOTESAll three need Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fRThe lp spooler, whose manual is\fBDOC/lp/spooler.doc\fR,\fRis the other spooler here..SH SEE ALSOlpsched, lpq